$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Compiler changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.atomic,Shared static constructors/destructors in templates now lower to `object._d_atomicOp`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.atomic.dd)
$(P
When a shared static constructor or destructor appears inside a template, it may
be instantiated in multiple modules, causing it to run multiple times. A shared
gate variable is used to count and guard executions, ensuring the body runs
exactly once across all modules. The atomic operation on that gate is necessary
because multiple threads may initialize modules concurrently.
)

$(P
This gate operation previously called `core.atomic.atomicOp` directly.
)

---
shared static this()
{
    if (atomicOp!"+="(gate, 1) != 1) return;
    // inside a template instantiation
}
---

$(P
It now lowers to `object._d_atomicOp`, consistent with how other compiler-generated
runtime hooks are handled.
)

---
if (.object._d_atomicOp!"+="(gate, 1) != 1) return;
---
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.cov-inline-pragma,Coverage counters are now emitted at call sites of inlined functions)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.cov-inline-pragma.dd)
$(P
Functions marked `pragma(inline, true)` (and functions inlined via the
`-inline` flag) would always show `0000000` in `-cov` coverage reports
even when executed. The inlined function body is never called as a
standalone function at runtime - only the inlined copy inside the caller
runs, so its coverage counters stayed at zero.
)

$(P
Coverage counters are now emitted at each call site where a function is
inlined, so the function's lines correctly reflect how many times they
were executed.
)

$(P
Fixes $(LINK2 https://issues.dlang.org/show_bug.cgi?id=5848, Issue 5848) /
$(LINK2 https://github.com/dlang/dmd/issues/18337, #18337)
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.ctfe_attribute,Added `@__ctfe` attribute for compile-time function enforcement)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.ctfe_attribute.dd)
$(P
A new `@__ctfe` attribute has been added to mark functions that can only be executed
at compile time. Functions marked with `@__ctfe` cannot be called from runtime code and
will not generate any object code.
)

$(P
When a `@__ctfe` function is used in a runtime context, the compiler will emit an error:
)

```d
int add(int a) @__ctfe => a + 2;

void main() {
    int x = add(9); // Error: function add marked with @__ctfe cannot be called at runtime
}
```

$(P
The attribute is useful for defining helper functions that should only be evaluated
during compilation, ensuring they don't contribute to binary size or runtime overhead:
)

```d
@__ctfe int square(int x) => x * x;

enum Result = square(5); // OK - evaluated at compile time
static assert(Result == 25);
```

$(P
Taking the address of a `@__ctfe` function in runtime code is also an error:
)

```d
int add(int a) @__ctfe => a + 2;

void main() {
    auto fp = &add; // Error: cannot take address of function marked with @__ctfe
}
```
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.deprecations,Many deprections have been turned in errors)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.deprecations.dd)
$(P
Using `return` statements in scope guards is now an error (in addition to contracts and `try...finally`)
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.fastdfa.escapeanalysis,The fast DFA engine has gained escape analysis capabilities)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.fastdfa.escapeanalysis.dd)
$(P
The engine works on the fundamental concept on "cells", a cell is a location in memory that holds something.
So a variable that is on the stack has a cell that provides it, but also may point to another cell if its typed as a pointer.
)

$(P
Due to the fast DFA engine not being able to model indirection, the cell of a variable and its containing pointer is considered separately from cells seen via indirection.
This is particularly interesting with how by-ref parameters handle it.
The cell pointed at by the by-ref is the outer cell, even if its typed as a pointer.
)

```d
// Think of parameter as int* not int, so the outer cell is the container for the int.
int* pointToRefCell(ref int arg) => &arg;
```
$(P
This allows you to escape values that come from indirection:
)

```d
struct Animal {
    int* datem;
}

int* grabFromAnimal(scope Animal* animal) => animal.datem;
```

$(P
Partial violations of ``scope`` is allowed in non-@safe functions.
Escaping via a throw statement will still error.
)

$(P
The first 29 parameters may be treated as outputs, all others must be inputs only.
This does not include the this pointer.
)

$(P
No attributes have been added at this time for users to use, you must rely solely on existing ones and inference.
)

$(P
Experimentally it may promote stack variables that are typed as a class to stack automatically.
This may be disabled in the future if it is shown to cause problems.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.foreach-shadow-error,Foreach variable shadowing is now an error)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.foreach-shadow-error.dd)
$(P
A deprecation introduced in DMD 2.089.0 for foreach variables shadowing
outer scope symbols has been upgraded to an error.
)

$(P
This affects both variables declared inside a foreach body that shadow
outer variables, and duplicate variable names in foreach parameter lists
when using `opApply`.
)

---
void main()
{
    int[int] arr;
    int x;
    foreach (i, j; arr)
    {
        int x; // Error: variable `x` is shadowing variable `...x`
    }
}
---

---
struct Foo
{
    int opApply(scope int delegate(size_t, size_t, ref uint)) => 0;
}

void main()
{
    foreach (x, y, x; Foo()) {} // Error: variable `x` is shadowing variable `...x`
}
---
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.ftime-trace-inline,`-ftime-trace`: add instrumentation for the inliner pass)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.ftime-trace-inline.dd)
$(P
The `-ftime-trace` output previously had no coverage for the inliner pass.
When compiling with `-inline`, the time spent scanning and inlining functions
did not appear in the trace at all.
)

$(P
Two new spans are now emitted: `Inlining` covers the entire inliner pass, and
`Inline: <function>` covers each function scanned individually.
These are visible in trace viewers like $(LINK2 https://ui.perfetto.dev/, Perfetto).
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.ftime-trace-sema-subspans,`-ftime-trace`: break up the template instance span into sub-phases)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.ftime-trace-sema-subspans.dd)
$(P
The `-ftime-trace` output previously showed template instantiation as a single
flat block, making it hard to tell where the time was actually going.
)

$(P
The `Sema1: Template Instance` span now contains sub-spans for each phase:
`Sema1: Template Arg Semantic`, `Sema1: Overload Resolution`,
`Sema1: Template Members`, `Sema2: Template Instance`, and `Sema3: Template Instance`.
These are visible in trace viewers like $(LINK2 https://ui.perfetto.dev/, Perfetto).
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.importc-c23-single-arg-static-assert,ImportC supports single-argument `_Static_assert(expr)`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.importc-c23-single-arg-static-assert.dd)
$(P
C23 makes the message operand of a static assertion optional.
ImportC now accepts both forms:
)

-------
_Static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4);
_Static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4, "unexpected int size");
-------

$(P
The two-argument form continues to work as before. When the assertion
fails and no message was supplied, the diagnostic reports that the
condition is false.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.importc-construct-consideration-pragmas,ImportC pragmas for C construct consideration)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.importc-construct-consideration-pragmas.dd)
$(P
ImportC now has a number of pragmas which may be used to alter
whether ImportC considers or ignores declarations and definitions with specific identifiers.
)

$(P
Those pragmas are:
- `#pragma function_decl(ignore, <identifier>,...)`
- `#pragma function_decl(consider, <identifier>,...)`
- `#pragma function_def(ignore, <identifier>,...)`
- `#pragma function_def(consider, <identifier>,...)`
)

$(P
These pragmas are for situations where it is desirable for ImportC to look past
a C declaration/definition of a symbol, and to instead use a declaration/definition
from an `__import`ed D module.
)

$(P
For example, a small subset of a C library's functions may rely
on non-standard compiler extensions that are unsupported by ImportC;
in such a situation `#pragma function_def(ignore, <identifier>,...)`
can be used to ignore the offending functions so that they can
instead be implemented in D and made available to ImportC via an `__import`ed D module.
)

$(P
Likewise, a C library may rely on externally-defined functions
which are not defined when compiling via ImportC;
`#pragma function_decl(ignore, <identifier>,...)` can be used to ignore the function declarations
and those functions can instead be implemented in D and `__import`ed in C.
)

$(P
$(HR)
)

$(P
The Construct Consideration pragmas are used as follows:
- `#pragma function_decl(ignore, <identifier>,...)`
- `#pragma function_decl(consider, <identifier>,...)`
- `#pragma function_def(ignore, <identifier>,...)`
- `#pragma function_def(consider, <identifier>,...)`
)

$(P
`#pragma function_decl` is used to alter whether ImportC ignores or considers (stops ignoring) *function declarations*
with the same name as any of the identifiers supplied to the pragma.
)

$(P
`#pragma function_def` is used to alter whether ImportC ignores or considers (stops ignoring) *function definitions*
with the same name as any of the identifiers supplied to the pragma.
)

$(P
For example:
)
```c
// We start ignoring function-declarations and function-definitions of `foo` and `bar`.
#pragma function_decl(ignore, foo, bar)
#pragma function_def(ignore, foo, bar)
// This declaration of `foo` is ignored.
void foo(int);
// As is this definition of `foo`.
void foo(int x)
{}

// We stop ignoring function-definitions of `bar`.
#pragma function_def(consider, bar)
// This declaration of `bar` is ignored.
void bar(int);
// This definition of `bar` is not ignored.
float bar(float x, float y)
{
    return x * y;
}
```
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.monitor-field,The `Object.__monitor` field has been moved to druntime)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.monitor-field.dd)
$(P
This field was previously hard coded into the compiler, and is used as a mutex for `synchronized(obj)` blocks.
By declaring it explicitly in druntime's `Object`, custom druntimes can now omit it entirely, saving 8 bytes per class instance when `synchronized` is not needed.
)

$(P
The field is treated specially by the compiler and remains excluded from:
)

$(P
- `__traits(allMembers, Object)`
- `Object.tupleof`
- `__traits(getPointerBitmap, T)` (it is not GC-managed)
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.more-wasi-reserved-versions,`WASIp1`, `WASIp2`, and `WASIp3` are now reserved version identifiers)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.more-wasi-reserved-versions.dd)
$(P
New version identifiers are reserved for WASI, corresponding to previews 1, 2, and 3 of the WebAssembly System Interface.
)

$(P
The existing `WASI` identifier is retained, meaning ANY version of WASI. i.e. `WASI` is always defined whenever `WASIp{1|2|3}` is.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.named-args-ifti,Implicit Function Template Instantiation (IFTI) handled named arguments better)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.named-args-ifti.dd)
$(P
https://github.com/dlang/dmd/issues/21335
https://github.com/dlang/dmd/issues/22878
)

$(P
When calling a function template with named arguments, parameters that are
skipped (because their named argument was not provided) now correctly use
their template parameter defaults for type deduction.
)

---
void f(A = int, B = int)(A a = A.init, B b = B.init) {}

void main()
{
    f(b: "hello"); // A = int (template default), B = string (deduced)
}
---

$(P
Named arguments that appear behind variadic arguments can now be assigned to:
)

---
void error(T...)(T args, string file = __FILE__, int line = __LINE__) {}

auto text(T...)(T args, Allocator alloc) {}

void foo(Allocator gc)
{
    error("Code: ", code, file: __FILE__, line: __LINE__)

    return text("hello", "world", alloc: gc);
}
---
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.newpdb,Improved CodeView / PDB symbolic debug info on Windows)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.newpdb.dd)
$(P
The Windows CodeView / PDB debug information emitter (used with `-g` when producing MS-COFF object files) now emits additional modern records expected by Microsoft and LLVM debugging tools. These improvements are enabled by default, so debugging with Visual Studio, WinDbg and LLVM-based tools works better out of the box.
)

$(P
The following are now generated:
)

$(P
$(LIST
    * `S_OBJNAME` and `S_COMPILE3` compiland records reporting the configured language and the real compiler version string,
    * `S_ENVBLOCK` and `S_BUILDINFO` (`LF_BUILDINFO`) records describing the build environment,
    * `S_FRAMEPROC` records describing each function's stack frame,
    * `LF_UDT_SRC_LINE` records recording the source file and line where each user-defined type (struct, class, enum) is defined, so debuggers can jump to a type's definition,
    * blake3 source-file checksums in the file-checksums subsection so debuggers can verify the source matches.
)
)

$(P
No action is required; simply compile with `-g` as before:
)

```console
dmd -g myapp.d
```
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.qstring-crlf,Token strings now normalize `\r\n` to `\n`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.qstring-crlf.dd)
$(P
Token strings (`q{...}`) now normalize carriage return + line feed sequences to
a single line feed, consistent with other string literal types.
)

```d
const string s = q{a
b}; // file saved with CRLF line endings
static assert(s == "a\nb");
```
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.struct-rvalue-assign,Disallow discarding an assignment to a struct rvalue)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.struct-rvalue-assign.dd)
$(P
It has always been an error for $(DDSUBLINK spec/struct, POD, POD) structs
to assign from an rvalue. It is now an error to discard the result of an
assignment from a struct rvalue when it would call `opAssign`, `opOpAssign`,
`opUnary!"++"`, or `opUnary!"--"` and the struct has no tail-mutable pointer
fields:
)

---
struct S
{
    int i;

    void opAssign(S s);
}

S foo();

void main()
{
    foo() = S(2); // Error, possible no-op
}
---

$(P
Above, unless `opAssign` mutates global data, the assignment in `main`
will have no effect and indicates a bug.
)

$(P
If a struct rvalue assignment is needed to mutate global state, either
call the operator overload method directly or use an lvalue.
Note: Calling a non-const method on a struct rvalue
$(DDSUBLINK spec/struct, member-functions, is allowed).
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.tuple-unpacking,Tuple unpacking is now supported!)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.tuple-unpacking.dd)
$(P
D now supports tuple unpacking for variable declarations, `foreach` loops, and function literal
template parameters.
This feature is available as a preview and can be enabled with the `-preview=tuples` compiler switch.
It allows extracting multiple values from a tuple or compile-time sequence directly into distinct variables.
)

-------
// Requires -preview=tuples
import std.typecons : tuple;

void main()
{
    auto (x, y, z) = tuple(1, 2.5, "three");
    assert(x == 1);
    assert(y == 2.5);
    assert(z == "three");

    // Types can be specified explicitly
    (int a, string b) = tuple(4, "five");

    // Unpacking works in foreach loops
    foreach ((i, s); [tuple(1, "one"), tuple(2, "two")])
    {
        // ...
    }
}
-------

$(P
For more information, see the D blog article: $(LINK2 https://blog.dlang.org/2026/04/12/dip-1053-a-tale-of-tuples/, DIP 1053 - A Tale of Tuples).
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.ucrt-runtime,UCRT is now used for the C runtime on Windows)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.ucrt-runtime.dd)
$(P
DMD on Windows now uses the Universal CRT (UCRT) for its C runtime in all cases.
)

$(P
For installations with Visual Studio 2015 (or later) or the Windows 10 SDK, the default
C runtime is unchanged: it remains $(TT libcmt), which statically links the UCRT.
)

$(P
When no such Visual C installation is found, DMD now falls back on the UCRT-based
libraries bundled in the MinGW folder shipped with DMD ($(TT ucrtbase.lib) together with
$(TT vcruntime140.lib)), instead of the old $(TT msvcrt120) runtime which did not support
modern format specifiers such as `%zd`. This fallback is fully supported.
)

$(P
If no UCRT-capable toolchain is detected and no $(TT -mscrtlib) switch is given, DMD now
reports a clear error instead of silently producing a non-working link.
)

$(P
Detection of Visual Studio versions older than 2015 (2008 - 2013) is deprecated, because
those versions predate the UCRT. Falling back on such an installation emits a deprecation
warning.
)

$(P
The $(TT -mscrtlib) switch is unchanged and can still be used to select any C runtime,
including $(TT msvcrt) (dynamic), the debug variants $(TT libcmtd) / $(TT msvcrtd), or a
legacy $(TT msvcrtNNN) runtime.
)
)


)

$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Runtime changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 druntime.importc-initial-msvc-intrinsics,ImportC implements the vast majority of MSVC's intrinsics: 461 of them to be exact.)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/druntime.importc-initial-msvc-intrinsics.dd)
$(P
ImportC now implements the vast majority of MSVC's intrinsics: 461 of them to be exact.
All but three of the intrinsics [listed here](https://web.archive.org/web/20240412171516/https://learn.microsoft.com/en-ie/cpp/intrinsics/alphabetical-listing-of-intrinsic-functions?view=msvc-170) are implemented, alongside a handful of undocumented intrinsics, accompanied by a smattering of ISA-specific intrinsics.
)

$(P
The presence of these intrinsics greatly improves ImportC's ability to successfully import C headers when targeting Windows.
Notably, `Windows.h` can now be successfully included by ImportC.
)

$(P
To make use of MSVC intrinsics in ImportC, simply `#include` the `importc_msvc_builtins.h` header before any C code that uses MSVC intrinsics:
)
```c
#include &lt;importc_msvc_builtins.h&gt;
#include &lt;windows.h&gt;
```
$(P
MSVC intrinsics are available when targeting the Microsoft C runtime (that is, when the `CRuntime_Microsoft` version identifier is defined).
)

$(P
Every intrinsic implemented has received optimised code for LDC, GDC, and DMD.
Each intrinsic, where possible, has a CTFE-compatible code path.
)

$(P
(Owing to the newness of DMD's AArch64 backend, MSVC intrinsics have not yet been implemented for DMD AArch64 targets. They have been implemented for LDC and GDC AArch64 targets.)
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 druntime.pow,Translate `^^` operator to druntime hook)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/druntime.pow.dd)
$(P
The exponentiation operator (`^^`) is now lowered to a druntime hook call instead of being expanded inline by the compiler.
This change moves the implementation logic into the runtime library, allowing for easier maintenance and platform-specific optimizations without requiring compiler modifications.
)

```d
auto _d_pow(Base, Exp)(Base base, Exp exp);
auto _d_sqrt(T)(T x);
```
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 druntime.timerfd,`core.sys.linux.timerfd` moved to `core.sys.linux.sys.timerfd`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/druntime.timerfd.dd)
$(P
Usually, a linux header that's included in C like `#include <sys/time.h>` gets its translation in `core/sys/linux/sys/time.d`.
timerfd.d was an exception, not being put in the `sys` package.
The module has been moved there, and the old module still exists as a deprecated module that publicly imports the new module.
)

---
import core.sys.linux.timerfd; // deprecated
import core.sys.linux.sys.timerfd; // corrective action
---
)


)

$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Library changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 gc_heap_mallocator,New `GCHeapMallocator` allocator in `std.experimental.allocator`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE phobos, changelog/gc_heap_mallocator.dd)
$(P
$(REF_ALTTEXT `GCHeapMallocator`, GCHeapMallocator, std, experimental, allocator, gc_allocator)
allocates from the D runtime's garbage-collected heap, but expects the memory to
be released manually via `deallocate` instead of leaving it for the collector.
The memory is still scanned for pointers by the GC.
)

$(P
It offers the same allocation, reallocation and deallocation behaviour that
$(REF_ALTTEXT `GCAllocator`, GCAllocator, std, experimental, allocator, gc_allocator)
has today, under a name that says so explicitly.
)

-------
import std.experimental.allocator.gc_allocator : GCHeapMallocator;

auto buffer = GCHeapMallocator.instance.allocate(1024 * 1024 * 4);
scope(exit) GCHeapMallocator.instance.deallocate(buffer);
-------
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 getopt_callback_index,getopt: add callback handler with element index)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE phobos, changelog/getopt_callback_index.dd)
$(P
This adds a new callback handler variant to getopt.
The handler is of the form $(D (string option, string value, size_t index)).
The $(D index) is the position of the currently processed option
in the argument array passed to getopt.
This enables getopt to handle positional arguments.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 specialcasing,Language dependent casing rules are now implemented in std.uni)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE phobos, changelog/specialcasing.dd)
$(P
The functions ``toLowerSpecial`` and ``toUpperSpecial`` provide case conversion when a language Lithuanian, Turkish or Azeri is provided to it that follows that languages rules.
)

$(P
This solves the problem of trying to make the letter ``i`` upper case into the Turkish ``İ``.
)
)


)

$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Dub changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 ldc-conf,Fixed `--conf` options not being passed to LDC's linker step.)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dub, changelog/ldc-conf.dd)
$(P
Packages can now use `--conf` as a dflag to modify LDC's linker
behavior for a package.
)

$(P
Example:
)
-------
dflags "--conf=$KON_PACKAGE_DIR/55-wasm-wasi.conf" platform="wasm32"
-------
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 ninja-generator,Added `dub generate ninja` command.)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dub, changelog/ninja-generator.dd)
$(P
Introduces a Ninja build backend for DUB. Running `dub generate ninja`
produces a `build.ninja` file with compile and link rules for all source
files in the project, using the correct flag syntax for DMD, LDC, and GDC.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 windows-response-file-escape,Fix Windows response file quoting for paths with trailing backslashes)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dub, changelog/windows-response-file-escape.dd)
$(P
When dub writes spaced compiler flags into `@` response files, a trailing
directory separator before the closing quote could escape that quote under
DMD/LDC response expansion. This broke ldc2 builds for users whose dub cache
paths contain spaces (regression since import paths gained trailing slashes).
)

$(P
Related: ldc-developers/ldc#5134
)
)


)

$(CHANGELOG_SEP_TEXT_BUGZILLA)

